1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,510
Every file on Linux decides who can read it, change it, or run it.

2
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:09,703
Get permissions wrong, and nothing works, or worse, everything is open to everyone.

3
00:00:10,173 --> 00:00:15,849
In this video: list, set, and change standard ugo/rwx permissions.

4
00:00:20,369 --> 00:00:22,952
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

5
00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:28,010
List, set, and change standard ugo/rwx permissions.

6
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:32,043
We'll read permissions, change them with letters and with numbers,

7
00:00:32,063 --> 00:00:35,734
see what happens when we remove one, and see how directories differ.

8
00:00:43,254 --> 00:00:49,321
Permissions are shown by ls -l. First, a folder called perms, and a file inside it.

9
00:00:49,341 --> 00:00:53,282
mkdir, and echo with the greater-than sign, as before.

10
00:00:59,752 --> 00:01:04,632
ls -l prints one line per file. The first column holds the permissions:

11
00:01:04,652 --> 00:01:06,772
ten characters, and then a dot.

12
00:01:12,292 --> 00:01:15,905
Here is how to read it. The first character is the type:

13
00:01:15,925 --> 00:01:20,121
a dash for a file, d for a directory. Then three groups of three:

14
00:01:20,141 --> 00:01:25,375
the user, who owns the file, then the group, then others, which is everyone else.

15
00:01:25,395 --> 00:01:28,229
The dot is an SELinux marker, a later topic.

16
00:01:31,699 --> 00:01:35,873
Each group is r, w, and x, or a dash when it is off.

17
00:01:35,893 --> 00:01:40,632
Read is 4, write is 2, execute is 1. Add them up per group.

18
00:01:40,652 --> 00:01:46,762
r w dash is 4 plus 2, which is 6. r dash dash is 4. So this file is 6, 4, 4.

19
00:01:53,282 --> 00:01:55,091
chmod changes permissions.

20
00:01:55,111 --> 00:01:59,243
With letters, you write who, then plus or minus, then what.

21
00:01:59,263 --> 00:02:01,494
u plus x gives the user execute.

22
00:02:01,514 --> 00:02:05,294
The letters are u for user, g for group, o for others.

23
00:02:11,764 --> 00:02:15,755
g plus w gives the group write. Watch the middle trio change.

24
00:02:20,225 --> 00:02:24,386
o minus r takes read away from others. Now others have nothing at all.

25
00:02:29,906 --> 00:02:36,307
With numbers, you set all three groups at once. 640 means user 6, group 4, others 0:

26
00:02:36,327 --> 00:02:40,894
r w dash, r dash dash, and nothing. The listing confirms it.

27
00:02:45,364 --> 00:02:49,262
755 is the classic for programs and directories:

28
00:02:49,282 --> 00:02:54,486
the user gets everything, group and others get read and execute.

29
00:02:54,506 --> 00:02:56,608
r w x, r dash x, r dash x.

30
00:03:02,128 --> 00:03:06,760
What do the bits actually do? Take read away from the owner with u minus r.

31
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:08,807
Now cat fails: Permission denied.

32
00:03:14,277 --> 00:03:17,371
Give it back with u plus r, and cat prints hello again.

33
00:03:21,891 --> 00:03:25,968
Directories use the same letters, but x means something different:

34
00:03:25,988 --> 00:03:27,954
it lets you enter the directory.

35
00:03:27,974 --> 00:03:32,858
We make a folder called secret with a file inside, then remove x from the user.

36
00:03:39,328 --> 00:03:41,343
cd fails: Permission denied.

37
00:03:41,363 --> 00:03:46,795
ls -ld, with the d option, shows the folder itself instead of its contents.

38
00:03:46,815 --> 00:03:51,084
It starts with d for directory, and the user part has no x.

39
00:03:57,554 --> 00:04:01,502
Put x back, and the contents are reachable again: there is a.txt.

40
00:04:06,022 --> 00:04:09,458
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

41
00:04:10,978 --> 00:04:14,564
Permissions are stored with the file, so they survive a reboot.

42
00:04:14,584 --> 00:04:16,910
We restart the lab, and it comes back up.

43
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:25,705
The file is still 755, and the practice file we made is still 640.

44
00:04:25,725 --> 00:04:28,701
Same permissions, after a fresh boot.

45
00:04:36,221 --> 00:04:40,161
Your turn. In your home directory, make a folder lab10.

46
00:04:40,181 --> 00:04:43,689
Inside it, create notes.txt with permissions 640,

47
00:04:43,709 --> 00:04:47,145
and a folder called shared with permissions 750.

48
00:04:50,615 --> 00:04:55,288
Check with ls -l. Run the checker, and try before you open the solution.

49
00:05:01,808 --> 00:05:07,041
Next up: Locate, read, and use system documentation, including man, info,

50
00:05:07,061 --> 00:05:10,279
and files in /usr/share/doc.

51
00:05:10,749 --> 00:05:13,651
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
